# Coreflux conventions

- **LoT is the only configuration language** for actions, models, routes, rules, panels and
  themes. Never propose JSON/YAML for these. Read the `lot-authoring` skill before writing LoT.
- **Talk to the broker through the `coreflux-broker` MCP tools**, not ad-hoc shell MQTT
  clients: `broker_command`, `broker_overview`, `lot_lint`, `lot_deploy`, `lotnb_deploy`,
  `mqtt_publish`, `mqtt_subscribe`, `mqtt_read_retained`, `broker_action_trace`,
  `broker_route_template`, `project_upload`, `project_export`, `broker_connection`.
  If no broker is reachable, still lint, and hand the user the exact `-command` payload.
- **Lint before deploy, verify after deploy.** `lot_lint` → deploy → read every envelope
  (`success`, `errors[]`) → prove the effect with `broker_action_trace` / `mqtt_subscribe` /
  route `status`. Never claim something works without seeing it on a topic or in an envelope.
- **Broker error messages win.** When a parse error comes back, fix the LoT to satisfy the
  parser; do not retry the same text.
- **Destructive commands need an explicit request from the user in this conversation:**
  `-removeAll*`, `-removeProject`, `-unloadProject`, `-removeUser`, `-restoreRules`,
  `-changeUserPassword`, `-changeUserSettings`, `-loadLicense`. State the consequence first.
- **Credentials never appear in LoT or notebooks.** Use `GET ENV` / `GET SECRET` and tell the
  user which `-setEnv` / `-setSecret` names to create. Never echo a secret value.
- **Test traffic is real traffic.** Before `mqtt_publish` on a topic that routes or actions
  consume on a non-local broker, say what will be published and why.
- **Docs:** search the `coreflux-docs` MCP server (docs.coreflux.org) for route settings,
  syntax you are unsure of, or version-specific behaviour, and cite the page.
- Write "LoT" in prose, `lot` as the code-fence language; `$SYS/Coreflux/…` topics are
  case-sensitive; topic positions and query placeholders are 1-based.
